  "notes": "The two plates were transcribed separately with the guess that they were two floors of one house - the basement's spine passage is 35 by 10 and so is the ground floor's hall, and the area wells line up. The specification on page 373 settles it: the same kitchen, scullery, servants' hall, butler's room, larder and pantry and store room, at the same sizes. So the two records are now one, and the first floor and the two rooms in the roof come from the specification. The specification gives the drawing and dining rooms as 13 by 15 where the plate draws them 30 by 15; the plate is recorded, since a 13-foot room does not fill the space it is drawn in. 2600 pounds. Plate 57 on page 87 draws the first floor, the roof and the two rooms in it, and its dimensions are the specification's: six bedrooms, five of them fifteen feet square, a bath room of eleven by ten and a cistern over the stair.",
